#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158091} :activity/id #uuid "f696f0f4-7c66-58b2-8b6c-9b62c56bed9c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048720 :topic/id #uuid "b86a089a-2daf-5959-af2d-4dfdfa1f55e6" :topic/math-academy-id 2927} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158432 :tutorial/content "We can extend a scalar function $f(x)$ to a square matrix argument $A.$ This function $f(A)$ is called a **matrix function**, and maps a square matrix to another square matrix of the same size.\n\nGiven a square matrix $A$ and a scalar function $f$ with Maclaurin series\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_k x^k,\n$$\n\nwe can define $f(A)$ by replacing $x$ with $A,$ with addition and multiplication performed in the matrix sense:\n\n$$\nf(A) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_k A^k\n$$\n\nFor example, recall that the Maclaurin series of $f(x) = e^x$ is\n\n$$\ne^x = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{x^k}{k!}.\n$$\n\nThen, for a square matrix $A,$ we define the matrix exponential as\n\n$$\ne^A = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{k!}A^k.\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we'll see how to apply matrix functions to square matrices." :tutorial/id #uuid "c3451824-f52b-539c-b16f-3819d3ea37df" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10688 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "215d5365-8c77-5734-bfe7-0ac93d99aa62" :step/math-academy-id 231906 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158433 :tutorial/content "Suppose $D$ is a diagonal matrix with diagonal entries $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor a scalar function $f$ with a Maclaurin series expansion, $f(D)$ is the diagonal matrix with diagonal entries $f(\\lambda_1), \\ldots, f(\\lambda_n){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({\\lambda }_{2}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the function $f(x) = e^x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis rule works because raising a diagonal matrix to a power is equivalent to raising each diagonal entry to the same power.\n\nIndeed, if\n$\\displaystyle\nf(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_k x^k$\nis the Maclaurin series of $f,$ then, by the definition of $f(D),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{D}^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1}^{k} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}^{k} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{1}^{k} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{2}^{k} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{n}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({\\lambda }_{2}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da162714-f641-5ded-aec8-6f24c0d5cbb3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10689 :tutorial/title "Function of a Diagonal Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "c8b4e298-ed67-50ea-bb52-8968e4cd58bf" :step/math-academy-id 231907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157268 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157036 :question/id #uuid "5cba0e13-0b34-568b-8a69-eac3fced6338" :question/math-academy-id "e-16515"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "343f8efc-fdef-51dc-ad3f-fad1ab37bc88" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050812}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292552}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Diagonal Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "91d86e1a-5d29-5149-9978-86770afc6de0" :step/math-academy-id 231908 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158434 :tutorial/content "Suppose $J_n(\\lambda)$ is an $n\\times n$ Jordan block where the main diagonal entries are all $\\lambda{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 1 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & \\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor a scalar function $f$ analytic at $\\lambda$ (i.e., has a Taylor series expansion at $\\lambda$), $f(J_n(\\lambda))$ is the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{''}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $f(J_3(2))$ for $f(x) = \\ln x.$\n\nSince $J_3(2)$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, we will need the first $3-1=2$ derivatives of $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}x \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{''}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/43/439d1a1c3c2c315510fd293e8bb8e1467e149222025359a0e0d899270eb07e12.svg)\n\nNow that we can compute the value of a function of a Jordan block, we can also find the value of a function of a Jordan matrix.\n\nThe matrix [math]f(J_n(\\lambda))[/math] for [math]n=2,3,[/math] and [math]4[/math] are as follows.\n\n* For [math]2\\times 2[/math] Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_2(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n* For [math]3\\times 3[/math] Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_3(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & 0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n* For [math]4\\times 4[/math] Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_4(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) & \\dfrac16f'''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & 0 & f(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & 0 & 0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]" :tutorial/id #uuid "7972371e-9205-5e74-b277-6dfcab332f9b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10691 :tutorial/title "Function of a Jordan Block"} :step/id #uuid "deee50a7-b19c-5fb9-80a5-a024ec3943c0" :step/math-academy-id 231911 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158424}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157269 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157037 :question/id #uuid "f2d8972a-4337-5608-9c82-6fb5e74d22c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-16516"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ce12d6f-8d1c-52f3-992f-534b9d91dda9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048640}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414006}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Block"} :step/id #uuid "c9bdde30-6bf2-5ffc-93a3-92c41bf6792d" :step/math-academy-id 231912 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157270 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157039 :question/id #uuid "2bd13883-8376-54f1-9985-83b95f1062a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-16522"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87883002-67eb-5111-b15a-c5941bb33f9b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414005}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f4444ff2-15d1-524f-952e-a3223b79ead3" :step/math-academy-id 231915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158435 :tutorial/content "Suppose $A$ and $S$ are matrices, where $S$ is invertible.\nFor a scalar function $f$ with a Maclaurin series expansion, we have\n\n$$\nf(SAS^{-1}) = Sf(A)S^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIndeed, given the Maclaurin series $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_kx^k,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(SA{S}^{-1}) & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}(SA{S}^{-1})^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}S{A}^{k}{S}^{-1} \\\\  & =S({\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{A}^{k}){S}^{-1} \\\\  & =Sf(A){S}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo far, we have seen how to evaluate functions for diagonal matrices and Jordan blocks. With the formula above, we will now be able to\n\n- evaluate $f(B)$ for diagonalizable $B$ using a diagonalization $B=PDP^{-1},$ and\n\n- evaluate $f(B)$ for non-diagonalizable $B$ using a Jordan decomposition $B=PJP^{-1}.$\n\nLet's see how with an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4241af18-972e-530c-b06b-388437001b24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10692 :tutorial/title "Function of a Jordan Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "82e8fc5c-326f-538e-acdc-fbdce35a6bb4" :step/math-academy-id 231918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157271 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157038 :question/id #uuid "6b751a5e-56c2-5ef0-a640-b77793aa1a72" :question/math-academy-id "e-16517"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049678}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414004}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "62349611-970a-5bd8-b8c7-3d773fb63239" :step/math-academy-id 231919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158466 :tutorial/content "Consider a Jordan matrix formed by the direct sum of blocks $J_{n_1}(\\lambda_1) {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} \\cdots {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} J_{n_k}(\\lambda_k)$ and a function $f.$ By applying the rule for diagonal matrices to this block diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1})\\,⊕\\,⋯\\,⊕\\,{J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})) & =f(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & {J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1})) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & f({J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1}))\\,⊕\\,⋯\\,⊕\\,f({J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to compute functions of any matrix in Jordan Normal Form by applying the function to each Jordan block individually.\n\nFor example, suppose $J = J_2(2) \\oplus J_1(3).$ Then, applying a function $f,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f({J}_{2}(2)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({J}_{1}(3))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e85abcea-5a44-50b9-9104-b71309622287" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14947 :tutorial/title "Function of a Jordan Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "b87874f9-2306-59b4-a907-b5f36d18a293" :step/math-academy-id 610975 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158095}}] :activity/title "Matrix Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157520}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414010} :activity/id #uuid "f7c04eb8-7724-552c-b148-1c56cf42932a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048720 :topic/id #uuid "b86a089a-2daf-5959-af2d-4dfdfa1f55e6" :topic/math-academy-id 2927} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f52e38a-24e3-5e4b-ac01-1a4c1fa10cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413971} :answer-field/id #uuid "093ddcaf-e7d3-52fa-b839-ddb952fa312d" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6955b1a9-a439-57c6-9b26-fa5bc116e904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413965} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e2ad92d-90c5-5fb3-835e-78716fc2952e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413987} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac63b60-993d-5f75-a07c-b064fe44237b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413975} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cd3c5e6-c84b-55d0-9b74-8cf6164e62a1" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413989} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e81f2645-ea93-5b69-8f12-9506be78d784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413982}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413982} :answer-field/id #uuid "67c84362-9ee8-5fb1-9cc5-4982acd43689" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7704adc1-0bd3-5a6a-8c14-5c138df4c7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413968} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7af99b-f3db-551f-a410-e7335484fbb7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6cea381-d89c-5a7b-a546-90c344bcd752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413979} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8825eea-6236-5c45-a6bf-fa0d2e334802" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da6ab59f-cfd7-5d6f-94dd-f3fddb0a05c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-253391" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{3}(2))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $n=3,$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\nSince $n=3,$ we need to compute the first $3-1=2$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "67945b3f-2216-5dac-be50-954f07e6800a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414011}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf34227-df92-59d8-9613-ceb5484789cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413956} :answer-field/id #uuid "25dde8d8-265c-54c9-ac3f-e40a69e95898" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c37bfdb-2dfe-5cc2-840b-243db54502a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413959}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413959} :answer-field/id #uuid "506c651f-f5b6-52a6-9177-275eb76f9e87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be1c477-86a4-5471-a907-e291cdd9c2e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413957} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fabe0ba-2f06-5d33-ae09-be09b22d98ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0ade4ad-0a6f-502d-8256-a7ee48405353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413981} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4498fa0-0077-5245-b464-34ae73a87b7d" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413996} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb01895-358d-5093-891c-8725486935f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413960} :answer-field/id #uuid "e436c140-e683-55ba-81fe-1b3bbe91bf3f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c827e8bb-8488-5496-9b36-15035f1d576c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413980} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c61432-7357-5400-9143-63f15ef64c3f" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7819c8fe-793f-5300-ba05-a00d3d72cc5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-253884" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))} & ={e}^{(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{1}(3) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(0)\\end{bmatrix})} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{({J}_{3}(0))}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{({J}_{1}(3))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{3}(0))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(3),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(3)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(0),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(0)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(0) & {f}^{′}(0) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(0)}{2} \\\\ 0 & f(0) & {f}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(0)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & {e}^{0} & \\frac{{e}^{0}}{2} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{0} & {e}^{0} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{0}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))} & ={e}^{({J}_{1}(3))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{3}(0))} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7697fba1-0f90-5fc4-8a3d-0c36de70f971"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7017ca3d-0bfe-52d1-9050-c702cd83a55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413967} :answer-field/id #uuid "1be2dfa0-21be-544d-a1f5-2b91c1394fc1" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413984} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e684f7-c0dc-543a-9c28-178ef40b05b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413961} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bc444ac-61d1-5d9d-a293-e4884a3c6bdc" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ac65ddc-f414-5326-a3bc-41c77e6c0772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413966} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dfbf7ac-1f