{J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)={e}^{x},$ $n=3,$ and $\\lambda =1.$\n\nSince $n=3,$ we need to compute the first $3-1=2$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(1)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2!} \\\\ 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}e & e & \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0 & e & e \\\\ 0 & 0 & e\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9a30b151-2968-59bd-b00e-dd670ecca6ba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fe7ce25-0b30-58c2-bf63-9503ead7b997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2e & {e}^{3}-4e \\\\ e & {e}^{3}-2e\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413963} {:answer/id #uuid "771abe02-8516-59d0-acc3-5894347ab301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3}+2e & -4e \\\\ e & {e}^{3}-2e\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413969} {:answer/id #uuid "86191a40-b259-50e8-a428-ed6d4258f5b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3e & -4e \\\\ e & -e\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413972} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f65777-1fc0-5be0-b73c-a38fdbb7f26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3} & -4{e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{3} & -{e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413976} {:answer/id #uuid "c1499551-986a-5c17-a47d-1c94feaf79fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3{e}^{3} & 2{e}^{3} \\\\ 2{e}^{3} & -{e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413976} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc2ff6f6-b56f-5924-a7f7-5f180522f2b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e45b0296-d991-5cc8-b0d3-09c2315c742d" :question/math-academy-id "q-252923" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $B=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find ${e}^{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and the given matrix $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{B}={e}^{PJ{P}^{-1}}=P\\cdot ({e}^{J})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $3$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=\\begin{bmatrix}f(3) & {f}^{′}(3) \\\\ 0 & f(3)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{J}=\\begin{bmatrix}f(3) & {f}^{′}(3) \\\\ 0 & f(3)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3} & {e}^{3} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{B} & =P\\cdot ({e}^{J})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3} & {e}^{3} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & {e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{3} & -2{e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3} & -4{e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{3} & -{e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e83cd1cd-0cd6-5847-8ebe-90dbcdc74de7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086b5f8c-361e-5e84-ba7f-07908a6ffad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413955} {:answer/id #uuid "5f32c9ba-7882-5683-b822-c627858b4d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}72 & -52 \\\\ -166 & 75\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413962} {:answer/id #uuid "6915da6f-7547-5e67-bf4a-3730ded795b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}62 & 92 \\\\ -125 & 186\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413964} {:answer/id #uuid "774e359d-a732-5eac-9259-4f31c4ad4889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}32 & 24 \\\\ -18 & 180\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413970} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0c78fd-212a-50ef-8305-029fa98e0cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}31 & 46 \\\\ -67 & 98\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413955} :answer-field/id #uuid "d52e4f2e-ac8e-5bd0-a3c8-e99ceefdc963" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5a76ab5b-a059-5458-b5f4-e674816ed366" :question/math-academy-id "q-253488" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $X=PD{P}^{-1},$ find ${X}^{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={x}^{5}$ and the given matrix $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n{X}^{5}=(PD{P}^{-1})^{5}=P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n{D}^{5}=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{2}{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{5} & =P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -96 & 64\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f757eba5-179e-543c-86b9-1672886ff68d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414008}}] :activity/title "Matrix Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413954}]}
